>
> stevo
>
>
> On Mon, Sep 1, 2014 at 10:56 AM, Gleki Arxokuna <
> gleki.is.my.name@gmail.com> wrote:
>
>> Semantical categorization of the universe can't be done since most
>> concepts have more than one useful tag.
>> Look at this attempt:
>> http://mw.lojban.org/index.php?title=semantic_categorization_of_gismu
>> It is not perfect and no attempt can be perfect.
>>
>> Also read this:
>> http://www.alamut.com/subj/artiface/language/johnWilkins.html
>>
>>
>> 2014-09-01 18:36 GMT+04:00 TR NS <transfire@gmail.com>:
>>
>> I was thinking about stevo's `CV` categorized gismu (still would like to
>>> see that, btw) and recently reading about the notion of taxonomic languages
>>> (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philosophical_language) when a neat idea
>>> struck me. If the the first three letters of a gismu related all such words
>>> --for the sake of argument let's say all fruit words started with `fru`, so
>>> then `frupa` might mean apple, `furba` banana, `furca` strawberry, etc.
>>> then my idea is that there could be a special cmavo that created a "group
>>> context", call it `xi'o... xi'u` and then one could say thins like:
>>>
>>>     fruxi'o pa .e ba .e ca [xi'u]
>>>
>>> Which would be the same as
>>>
>>>     frupa .e fruba .e fruca
>>>
>>> Of course this cmavo is a little odd b/c it works more like an
>>> inflection. And unfortunately Lojban doesn't have any sort of taxonomic
>>> vocabulary whatsoever. So it doesn't really fly. But I thought it was a
>>> neat idea anyway, so I thought I'd share.
>>>
>>> .trans.
